# Build gate for a bridge on STM32F1 with a USART console, one downstream port
# on a second USART and one on the bxCAN controller.
#
# This is a gate, not the shape of a board: it exists to compile two things into
# a firmware and keep them compiling. The first is the bridge's CAN downstream
# port (CONFIG_BRIDGE_PORT_CAN, src/generic/bridge_can.c), the second is the CAN
# pipeline without the CANserial node on top of it - CONFIG_CANBUS here pulls the
# driver and generic/canbus.c, and nothing in this build selects CONFIG_CANSERIAL
# or CONFIG_MUX_CAN.
#
# It is not evidence that an F103 can carry USB and CAN at once: it cannot. On
# this part the USB peripheral and CAN1 RX0 share interrupt vector 20
# (USB_LP_CAN1_RX0_IRQn), so a bridge that carries a CAN port takes its console
# on a USART - here USART2 on PA3 (RX) / PA2 (TX) - with the host on the other
# side of a USB-UART adapter. CONFIG_LOW_LEVEL_OPTIONS is what puts that console
# choice on the menu; STM32_BRIDGE_CAN_PB8_PB9 itself refuses to combine with
# CONFIG_USBSERIAL on the F1, which says the same thing earlier and in the
# Kconfig rather than at link time.
#
# The ports: USART1 on PA10 (RX) / PA9 (TX) is the bridge's one UART slot, and
# the CAN controller on PB8 (RX) / PB9 (TX) is the slot after it, so
# BRIDGE_UART_COUNT is 2. No `.test` dictionary references this target.
CONFIG_MACH_STM32=y
CONFIG_MACH_STM32F103=y
CONFIG_LOW_LEVEL_OPTIONS=y
CONFIG_STM32_SERIAL_USART2=y
CONFIG_MUX_SERIAL=y
CONFIG_STM32_BRIDGE_SERIAL=y
CONFIG_STM32_BRIDGE_SERIAL_USART1=y
CONFIG_STM32_BRIDGE_CAN_PB8_PB9=y
